Im thinking on the commands of .docs/commands.md. I want you to discuss with me ways to implement them on the game. Could it be that each object (NPC, item, etc..) implement in them the function that
the different commands trigger (inventory & drop will be in the player, get will be in the object, ...). Does this complicate or simplify things? 




 Option 3: Hybrid Approach (RECOMMENDED)

  Combine both: Objects implement behavior, Engine orchestrates:

  # Objects implement their own logic
  class Item:
      def can_be_taken(self) -> tuple[bool, str]:
          """Check if item can be taken"""
          if self.fixed:
              return False, f"You can't take {self.name}. {self.reason_fixed}"
          return True, ""

      def on_taken(self, player, game_engine):
          """Called when item is successfully taken"""
          self.examined = True  # Auto-examine when picked up
          # Trigger any special effects

      def on_dropped(self, location):
          """Called when item is dropped"""
          pass

  # Engine orchestrates the flow
  class GameEngine:
      def _handle_get(self, interpretation) -> str:
          target = interpretation.get("target")

          # Ask the object if it can be taken
          can_take, reason = target.can_be_taken()
          if not can_take:
              return reason

          # Perform the action
          self.current_player.add_item(target, self)
          current_location = self.locations[self.current_player.current_location]
          current_location.remove_item(target.id)

          # Let the object react
          target.on_taken(self.current_player, self)

          # AI-enhanced response
          return self.ai_enhancer.enhance_action_result(
              action="get",
              target=target,
              success=True
          )

  ✅ Pros:

  1. Best of both worlds - Objects are smart, engine coordinates
  2. Flexible - Easy to add special item behaviors
  3. Readable - Clear separation of concerns
  4. Testable - Can test objects and engine separately
  5. Maintainable - Changes to item logic stay in Item class

  ❌ Cons:

  1. More complex - More methods to understand
  2. Convention needed - Need clear patterns (can_*, on_*, etc.)

  ---
  My Recommendation: Hybrid Approach

  Here's why:

  1. You already have some OO structure - Item.examine(), Item.use() exist
  2. Different objects need different logic - A locked door vs a gun vs a note
  3. Game design benefits - Easy to create special items with unique behaviors
  4. Maintains clean engine - Engine stays focused on orchestration

  Implementation Pattern:

  # Protocol/Interface for interactive objects
  class Interactive:
      def can_examine(self) -> tuple[bool, str]: ...
      def on_examine(self, context) -> str: ...

  class Takeable:
      def can_be_taken(self) -> tuple[bool, str]: ...
      def on_taken(self, player, engine): ...
      def on_dropped(self, location): ...

  class Usable:
      def can_be_used(self, target) -> tuple[bool, str]: ...
      def on_used(self, target, context) -> str: ...

  class Openable:
      def can_open(self, player) -> tuple[bool, str]: ...
      def on_opened(self, player): ...

  This way:
  - Engine = Traffic cop (routes commands, enforces game rules)
  - Objects = Smart actors (know their own capabilities and effects)
  - AI = Flavor enhancer (makes everything sound better)

